WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.100
For our second present, for the Second Day of Christmas,

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.100
we have two blankets.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.380
They are similar in some ways.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.460
I got one in July and the other one in December.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:27.820
So let's have a look at the first one.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:33.140
It's quite big and you can see I am not good at making beds.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:37.760
You can see, it's a present from the American Chemical Society.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:44.180
I gave a lecture in July to the Annual General Meeting of the Royal Society of Chemistry.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:47.480
They thought I'd make it a bit more exciting.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.860
The president of the American Chemical Society was there,

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:54.600
and a few weeks later, this came through the post...

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.140
...with a note saying that the president had loved my lecture and here was a present.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:04.140
It really is a blanket, and it's pretty up to date.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:07.300
(It's) got flerovium and livermorium.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.980
You can wear it, if you want, like a cloak

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.160
Brady: Oh yeah, like a chemical Jedi.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.420
Prof: That's the first one, let's lay it on the bench.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.480
The second one is quite similar.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.440
It is also a blanket,

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:29.260
but, it's actually what, in American, is called a quilt,

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:32.500
and in England is called an eiderdown.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.820
This is what you put on the top of your bed.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:37.180
My arms aren't quite long enough.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:43.060
And, this had been stitched by a special stitching machine.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:50.500
It looks as if it's hand made, but in fact, it's quite a cunning piece of computer programming.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:56.720
This was given to me by Theo Grey, who is famous for his periodic tables.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.560
He has a machine. I'm not quite sure what it's for,

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.240
but one of the things it can do is to produce these quilts.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:10.800
I had huge problems bringing this back on the train from London, where I was given it.

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:16.880
Eventually, I wrapped it up in a plastic sheet with a luggage strap 'round it.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:23.000
And I met a fan on the train, but I didn't have the courage to unwrap it.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.780
I think that these are both really nice.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.280
I haven't got the slightest idea what to do with them,

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:33.500
because I don't really want to have them on my bed.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:39.380
Well, I would like to, but I'm not sure other members of my family would be quite so keen.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:45.540
But, I think in the end we'll hang them up here in the School of Chemistry at Nottingham.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:50.060
On the Second Day of Christmas, we have two periodic table blankets,

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:56.140
on the First Day of Christmas, we had some tartaric acid from a wine barrel.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:02.040
Now, let's see what we're going to have on day three.

